Title: The schema-declaration leg cannot look without a fork checkout, and skips
Created: 2026-09-03
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-09-03T12:07Z claude created
2026-09-03T15:20Z claude note Leg 3 reads the reviewed SHA out of upstream.git (or a fork clone), never a working tree; not-run warns as well as skips; red, green and not-run controls landed

--- body ---

## Context

Filed by the v1.13.0 re-baseline (ticket 0618). The defect is not that a check
was wrong; it is that the one check that could have been right did not run, on
the exact day it was written for.

`UPSTREAM_INDEX_SCHEMA_VERSION` is declared in `UPSTREAM`, mirrored in
`bench/index_schema.mjs`, and stamped into the current-generation fixture by
`bench/fixtures/make_index_fixture.mjs`. `tests/test_index_schema_fixtures.py`
holds three legs over those three numbers:

1. the declaration exists and is numeric;
2. the declaration equals the fixture's stamp;
3. the mirror equals upstream's own `const SCHEMA_VERSION`.

Leg 2 is green by construction — the fixture is stamped *from* the mirror, so
the two agree whether or not either is right. Leg 3 is the only one that reads
anything upstream wrote, and it is guarded by

    if not source.exists():
        pytest.skip("no fork checkout — run `make upstream-checkout` to enable this leg")

`fork/` is gitignored and absent on a fresh clone. So on 2026-09-03, the day
upstream moved `SCHEMA_VERSION` from 1 to 2 for the first time since v1.7.0,
the suite reported **20 passed, 1 skipped** on a tree whose declaration was
wrong. That is a green that means "I could not look", which is the shape the
harness rules name and this repository has now filed five separate lessons
about.

The bump itself was caught by `make upstream-catchup`, which reads the constant
out of a bare mirror it maintains at `upstream.git/`. That is the point: a
source the check can always reach already exists in this tree, and the check
does not use it.

## Actions

1. Give leg 3 a source it can reach without a built checkout. `upstream.git/`
   is a bare mirror; `git -C upstream.git show <UPSTREAM_REVIEWED_SHA>:src/features/search/sqlite-index.ts`
   answers the question at the pinned commit, which is the *right* question —
   leg 3 as written reads whatever `fork/` happens to be checked out at.
2. When neither the mirror nor a checkout is present, the leg must report
   NOT-RUN rather than skip into a green. A skip and a pass are the same colour
   in a suite summary; that is the whole defect.
3. Keep leg 2. It is worthless alone and useful beside leg 3: together they say
   the fixture stamps what upstream stamps.

## Test

Red first, and the red is cheap because the tree was in it this morning: set
`UPSTREAM_INDEX_SCHEMA_VERSION` and the mirror to a generation upstream does
not declare, with no `fork/` present, and confirm the repaired leg fails where
the current one skips.

## Exit criteria
- [x] Leg 3 runs against the bare mirror at the reviewed SHA, with no `fork/`
- [x] With neither source present it reports not-run, distinguishably from pass
- [x] Demonstrated red against a deliberately wrong declaration, with no
      checkout, before the fix is trusted
